{
  "_comment": "Biomes. Ground is data first: the simulation reads a type and a movement number, the renderer reads a palette and a texture, and neither knows about the other. A BIOME is a country - its looks, its palette, how its land lies and what grows on it. A TYPE (data/terrain/terrain_types.json) is a rule - how fast a soldier crosses it - and a SOIL (data/terrain/soils.json) is the material underfoot. Every biome names the type its ground behaves as, so adding a snowfield never changes a movement number and re-balancing movement never changes how anything looks. When biomes meet, a tile carries WEIGHTS (70% grass, 30% forest) and colour, height, prop density, cover and movement all read those weights. Adding a biome is a block in this file: nothing in the generator knows the name of any one of them.",
  "_fields": {
    "id": "stable key, used by the world generator and by battlefields",
    "name": "what a player would call it",
    "type": "the terrain_types.json id whose movement rule this biome's ground follows",
    "grounds": "the CAMPAIGN MAP's four looks, each with four sub-variants: the look comes from the world (moisture, wear, region - the same field that decides the biome) and the sub-variant from a seeded hash of the tile's position. A tile always shows the same one, neighbours rarely match, and the four inside a look are rolled, mirrored or tone-shifted copies of one another - never cropped or resampled, because those break the seam.",
    "ground_scale": "how many world units one repeat of a campaign ground image covers",
    "palette": [
      "two to four colours, darkest first: what the height shading darkens and lightens from when there is no ground image"
    ],
    "height": {
      "base": "0..1 of the battlefield's height range",
      "roughness": "0 = flat, 1 = broken",
      "bias": "-1 hollows, +1 raises"
    },
    "terrain": "the BATTLEFIELD's own generation block. Everything the field generator reads lives here so a biome can be tuned - or written from scratch - without touching code.",
    "terrain.elevation": {
      "base": "0..1, the height the country sits at before anything is added to it",
      "amplitude": "multiplier on the config's elevation_amplitude: how tall this country's land gets",
      "roughness": "0 = table flat, 1 = broken ground. This is the LARGE scale - the base relief",
      "bias": "-1 sinks the country into hollows, +1 lifts it onto a plateau",
      "feature_scale": "multiplier on feature sizes: 1.0 is the default footprint, 2.0 doubles every hill"
    },
    "terrain.features": "how often each landform appears, per feature lattice cell. The lattice is spaced by feature_spacing cells, so these are densities, not counts, and they mean the same thing on a 100x60 field and on a 633x380 one.",
    "terrain.detail": "0..1, the restrained smaller-scale variation laid on afterwards. Kept low on purpose: a battlefield that is noisy everywhere is a battlefield formations cannot use.",
    "terrain.moisture": "0..1, what the ground is like - decides wetness, soil choice and the variant weights",
    "terrain.vegetation": "base density 0..1 plus per-kind densities (trees, bushes, rocks) that the cover and prop passes read",
    "terrain.soils": "which soils this biome may choose from, and their weights",
    "terrain.movement": {
      "vegetation_drag": "how much vegetation slows a body at full density",
      "wetness_drag": "how much standing water slows a body at full wetness",
      "slope_penalty": "how much a 1.0 (45 degree) slope costs, before the type and soil numbers are applied"
    },
    "terrain.variants": "the BATTLEFIELD's four ground variants. Same idea as grounds, at battle scale: Lush is not one texture but four that look the same and differ slightly, which is what stops repetition being visible. Which variant a cell shows comes from the field (moisture, wear, region), which sub-variant from a seeded hash of the repeat's position. Empty variant art falls back to the variant's dominant colour, so a biome with no art yet still renders.",
    "terrain.overlays": "up to four detail overlays per biome, painted on the ground by a coverage mask: dark grass, dry grass, dirt, stones, moss, flowers, dead vegetation, trampled ground. The mask is a channel (wetness, slope, wear, vegetation), never a scatter of isolated speckles. The first three become the shader's RGB channels and the fourth its alpha.",
    "terrain.props": "which prop kinds this biome grows and how thickly, per 100 tiles at full weight, with the minimum biome weight a tile needs before the prop appears at all",
    "prop_kinds": "the shared prop table: art, world size, and what the prop does to a battle (cover, sight line, obstruction). A biome names a kind and tunes its density, so a tree is a tree whether it is standing in plains or in swamp.",
    "type_looks": "the art for ground whose TYPE has its own look - water, rock and mud. These are shared by every biome rather than listed per biome: a river is a river. Empty art falls back to the type's debug colour, so a look that has not been made yet still renders."
  },

